            <ArticleTitle>The role of experiential avoidance in acute pain tolerance: a laboratory test.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>146-58</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The present investigation examined the role of experiential avoidance in terms of acute pain tolerance and subsequent recovery. Seventy nonclinical participants completed the Acceptance and Action Questionnaire and underwent a well-established cold pressor task. Results indicated that individuals reporting higher levels of experiential avoidance had lower pain endurance and tolerance and recovered more slowly from this particular type of aversive event. Consistent with theoretical prediction, these findings suggest that experiential avoidance may play a role in tolerance of acute pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
